A||N||12||BUhockey||Winter storage for an '88 Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||BUhockey||07-25-2001||09:57 AM||||I had an '88 Fiero V6 5-speed manual.... and I will be keeping it in the garage over the cool months here in New England (Boston area).... any advice????<P>The garage is unheated and I plan to "warm" the engine up about every weekend or so... maybe in a pinch every other weekend... this is an inherited Fiero and it has a great deal of sentimental value so I really want to take care of it..... Would an oil change in the spring every year be advisable even though I'm not putting 3k miles on it every year from spring-fall??? Should I change the place where the tires touch the ground in the garage every week (or every other week)??<P>------------------<BR>88 Fiero GT V6 5-speed manual<P>AOL IM: buhocky13<BR>MSN IM: buhockey13<BR>Yahoo IM: buhockey_13<BR>ICQ: 28847471, 28800545||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||07-25-2001||10:02 AM||||Well, if you're going to start it every week or so, no major precautions need to be taken, but I would jack the car up and support it with jackstands under the control arms.  Store the tires laying flat on their side, with cardboard, etc. between the tires so you can stack them one on top of the other.  That'll keep the tires from flatspotting.  When you get ready to start driving again, change the fluids, put the tires back on and you're good to go.||||reg||6
Z||000002||88CoupeV6||07-25-2001||10:39 AM||||Great, A MA fiero owner. You are lucky to have a garage.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I have to store my baby outside in winter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Do you belong to any Fiero clubs? We have a great and active club in the New England area. NEFA(New England Fiero Assotiation) <A HREF="http://WWW.Fieropride.com" TARGET=_blank>WWW.Fieropride.com</A>  Click on My Pics under my signature to see some pics from area events we have gone to. You would not want to miss our <FONT COLOR="#ff0000">Leaf Peepers Tour</FONT>, It is a blast. We also have a great newsletter.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jaygee79||07-25-2001||11:20 AM||||Welcome to the furum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I'm from MA too and I belong to NEFA.  Great club, we have lots of fun.  Kind of like our own little family  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jaime<BR>84SE red<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooh14.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||07-25-2001||04:28 PM||||Your going to be fine if your starting it up once a week.  Actually, once every other week probably is plenty.  I just let mine sit in the garage over the winter, and drive it occasionally as long as the roads are dry.  If it gets to be a long period of time I will run it in the driveway.<P>I used to put a set of tires on steel rims (they are all bald!) to let the car sit on to save the good tires.  This let me move the car if I needed the garage to work on a project or get something out of the loft.  Last winter I just left it on the regular tires because I was still driving it when the weather was good... and no flat spots.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||07-25-2001||10:46 PM||||All fluids, esp. engine oil should be fresh before putting the car in storage. Starting and running the car once a week may not be a good idea....there are differing opinions on this. I prefer not to run the car unless it can be driven in dry weather and brought up to full operating temp. If strage is to be long term, an application of a high quality oil spray rust inhibitor will go a very long way to keeping electrical problems away, as well as keeping rubber pieces in good shape. One last thing, a really nifty product, unfortunately I can't remember where to get them...it's like a ziploc bag for cars. You drive the car onto it before storage and just  zip it shut. The car is protected from moisture and stays spotlessly clean until you open the bag again.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||07-26-2001||12:00 AM||||I've got to get this page posted.....<P>1. Do Not put any type of 'preservative' or anything else on the belts or the brake lines. Many products contian oil and other chemicals that will actually damage belts and ruin brake lines.<P>2. Before you fill the tank, find Sta-Bil or another fuel stabilizer. Dump in enough to treat 10 gallons Then fill the tank. Run the car at least 10 minutes to circulate the fuel and stabilizer. This prevents crap gumming up. Especially important for port EFI and Carbed motors.<P>3. As mentioned above, All fluids should be fresh and full. In this case, Stuff the fuel tank. you want to keep air and moisture out of it. <P>There's one exception... Change the brake fluid as soon as you take it out of storage. The brake fluid draw water even when parked. That will get rid of it. No sense changing it before storing unless it's never been done. (If it's never been changed, doing it before storage will protect the brake hardware. Read my brake articles. Drive the car a few days to let the new fluid work into everything.)<P>4. If/when you run the motor, run it for No Less than 30 minutes, Peferably More. Weather permitting, it is best to drive it 30-60 minutes.<P>The reason is to heat the entire motor so that it will drive off any water that has condensed. It takes at least that long to heat up everything fully and charge the battery completely. Driving will fully heat the tranny and dry it out.<P>It will also stir up the antifreeze. Most people don't know that antifreeze will settle out of the mix. If you don't stir it up, the water on top is free to eat the radiator waterpump and heater core.<P>5. On a car stored outside,,, cover all 4 wheels with heavy canvas/tarp/etc. Tuck the canvas into the wheel well as best you can and weight/tie it down. This protects the wheels tires and the brakes from weather. Don't try to get it air tight... you don't want air to move under there so that condensation doesn't build up. Do this even if you use a car cover.<P>6. Cover the air grates in back. Even storing indoors. This will keep crap from falling down there.<P>7. Don't forget to check tire pressure at least once a month. If a tire goes flat don't leave it. The soft tire can relax away from the bead a bit and that will let corrosion start. The only way to fix a bead leak is to dismount the tire so you can clean and on AL wheels recoat them.<P>There's probably some things I forgot but this should cover the major items.||||reg||4
Z||000007||theogre||07-26-2001||12:02 AM||||I've got to get this page posted.....<P>1. Do Not put any type of 'preservative' or anything else on the belts or the brake lines. Many products contian oil and other chemicals that will actually damage belts and ruin brake lines.<P>2. Before you fill the tank, find Sta-Bil or another fuel stabilizer. Dump in enough to treat 10 gallons Then fill the tank. Run the car at least 10 minutes to circulate the fuel and stabilizer. This prevents crap gumming up. Especially important for port EFI and Carbed motors.<P>3. As mentioned above, All fluids should be fresh and full. In this case, Stuff the fuel tank. you want to keep air and moisture out of it. <P>There's one exception... Change the brake fluid as soon as you take it out of storage. The brake fluid draw water even when parked. That will get rid of it. No sense changing it before storing unless it's never been done. (If it's never been changed, doing it before storage will protect the brake hardware. Read my brake articles. Drive the car a few days to let the new fluid work into everything.)<P>4. If/when you run the motor, run it for No Less than 30 minutes, Peferably More. Weather permitting, it is best to drive it 30-60 minutes.<P>The reason is to heat the entire motor so that it will drive off any water that has condensed. It takes at least that long to heat up everything fully and charge the battery completely. Driving will fully heat the tranny and dry it out.<P>It will also stir up the antifreeze. Most people don't know that antifreeze will settle out of the mix. If you don't stir it up, the water on top is free to eat the radiator waterpump and heater core.<P>5. On a car stored outside,,, cover all 4 wheels with heavy canvas/tarp/etc. Tuck the canvas into the wheel well as best you can and weight/tie it down. This protects the wheels tires and the brakes from weather. Don't try to get it air tight... you don't want air to move under there so that condensation doesn't build up. Do this even if you use a car cover.<P>6. Cover the air grates in back. Even storing indoors. This will keep crap from falling down there.<P>7. Don't forget to check tire pressure at least once a month. If a tire goes flat don't leave it. The soft tire can relax away from the bead a bit and that will let corrosion start. The only way to fix a bead leak is to dismount the tire so you can clean and on AL wheels recoat them.<P>There's probably some things I forgot but this should cover the major items.||||reg||4
Z||000008||Formula88||07-26-2001||09:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Wannabe:<BR><B>All fluids, esp. engine oil should be fresh before putting the car in storage. Starting and running the car once a week may not be a good idea....there are differing opinions on this. I prefer not to run the car unless it can be driven in dry weather and brought up to full operating temp. If strage is to be long term, an application of a high quality oil spray rust inhibitor will go a very long way to keeping electrical problems away, as well as keeping rubber pieces in good shape. One last thing, a really nifty product, unfortunately I can't remember where to get them...it's like a ziploc bag for cars. You drive the car onto it before storage and just  zip it shut. The car is protected from moisture and stays spotlessly clean until you open the bag again.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Good point.  I think starting the car is fine, but like Wannabe said - make sure the car if fully warmed up.  I'd let it get up to full operating temp.  If everything's in good order, I'd run it until the cooling fan cycled a few time, just to be sure.  That helps to make sure it's warm enough to evaporate any condensation in the exhaust, etc.  You definately don't want to just fire it up for a minute or two and shut it down.  That'll actually be worst than not starting it at all, IMO.  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||Patrick's Dad||07-26-2001||10:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Great, A MA fiero owner. You are lucky to have a garage.  I have to store my baby outside in winter  Do you belong to any Fiero clubs? We have a great and active club in the New England area. NEFA(New England Fiero Assotiation) <A HREF="http://WWW.Fieropride.com" TARGET=_blank>WWW.Fieropride.com</A>  Click on My Pics under my signature to see some pics from area events we have gone to. You would not want to miss our Leaf Peepers Tour, It is a blast. We also have a great newsletter.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Welcome to the furum  I'm from MA too and I belong to NEFA. Great club, we have lots of fun. Kind of like our own little family <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow, I'm from MA, too, and never got a welcome like this.... Hey, B.U.Hockey, I guess we know who's the black sheep in <I>this</I> family....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
